  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ザボン」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B6%E3%83%9C%E3%83%B3",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110710178,
      "text": "ザボン は、柑橘類の一種である。ブンタン（文旦）の別名でも知られ、ほかにはボンタン、ウチムラサキ、ザンボア、ジャボンとも呼ばれる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pomelo」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomelo",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1369191012,
      "text": "The pomelo, also known as a shaddock, is the largest citrus fruit. It is an ancestor of several cultivated citrus species, including the bitter orange and the grapefruit. It is a natural, non-hybrid citrus fruit, native to Southeast Asia. Similar in taste to a sweet grapefruit, the pomelo is commonly eaten and used for festive occasions throughout Southeast and East Asia. As with the grapefruit, phytochemicals in the pomelo have the potential for drug interactions."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pomelo」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomelo#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1369191012,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The seeds of the pomelo are monoembryonic, producing seedlings with genes from both parents, so they do not breed true to type. However, they are usually fairly similar to the tree they grow from and therefore in Asia, pomelos are typically grown from seed. Seeds can be stored for 80 days at a temperature of 5 °C (41 °F) with moderate relative humidity. Pomelos can be propagated vegetatively by air-layering, by taking cuttings, by grafting, by shield budding, or by tissue culture.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
